WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:05.510
Dictator Muammar Gaddafi ruled Libya for more
than 40 years.

00:00:05.510 --> 00:00:09.500
But when the end came - it was fast.

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:14.820
In 2011, the people rose up in mass protests.

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:16.660
Rebels killed Gaddafi.

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:20.100
The dictator was gone.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:25.780
But the country has been in chaos ever since.

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:30.900
Fragmentation is the best single word to describe
what's happening in Libya.

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:38.539
A large number of actors and communities and
groups all effectively competing against each

00:00:38.539 --> 00:00:39.539
other.

00:00:39.539 --> 00:00:43.859
Militias, extremists and many international
powers are ripping the country apart.

00:00:43.859 --> 00:00:51.280
And there are even two rival administrations,
each claiming they should be in control.

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:55.960
So how did things get so bad - and so complicated
- in Libya?

00:00:55.960 --> 00:01:06.900
Let us unpack that for you.

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:10.830
First, let’s look at the bigger picture
- and what’s at stake.

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:16.320
Libya has a long coastline right at the doorstep
of the European Union.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:22.970
That has made it a major stepping stone for
people looking for refuge or a better life

00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:25.910
in the wealthy north.

00:01:25.910 --> 00:01:32.380
The government chasm in Libya has enabled
the rapid growth of things like human smuggling

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:33.380
through the country.

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:39.460
We've seen, of course, that this can allow
a breeding ground for extremist groups.

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:43.310
Libya is also home to Africa’s biggest oil
reserves.

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:47.250
And THIS puts it on the radar of other countries.

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:53.920
Any disruption of the energy supply coming
from Libya is also important for Europe.

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:58.080
But also because it constitutes the door to
Africa to some extent.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:05.000
So the country if the situation was better
could also play an important role as a platform

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:09.390
between Europe, Africa, the Middle East and
possibly further.

00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:13.680
And most importantly - there are six million
Libyan people.

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:17.109
Desperate for a safe, secure future.

00:02:17.109 --> 00:02:21.310
Thousands have been killed in the fighting,
hundreds of thousands have been displaced

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:27.040
from their homes, and more than a million
need humanitarian aid.

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:38.569
We can trace the roots of this situation all
the way back to 2011.

00:02:38.569 --> 00:02:44.159
The popular protests known as the Arab Spring
swept through the region.

00:02:44.159 --> 00:02:46.239
Dictators fell like dominos.

00:02:46.239 --> 00:02:50.310
And Libya’s strongman Gaddafi was no exception.

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:58.879
But the way he’d ruled made Libya ill equipped
to get back on its feet.

00:02:58.879 --> 00:03:04.200
what Gaddafi operationalised as a system…
is a divide and rule system to dilute the

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:11.659
relevance of anyone he would deem or any faction
he would deem potentially a political, military

00:03:11.659 --> 00:03:17.580
opponent, either by purging them or kind of
marginalizing them from his patronage network.

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:26.170
He used both his tribal knowledge and his
tribal skills, but he also used religion as

00:03:26.170 --> 00:03:30.330
well to control the population.

00:03:30.330 --> 00:03:35.599
Once Gaddafi was gone, tribes and militias
which had united to get rid of him - turned

00:03:35.599 --> 00:03:37.349
against each other.

00:03:37.349 --> 00:03:46.079
In 2014, a disputed election plunged Libya
into the abyss.

00:03:46.079 --> 00:03:54.879
Key civil institutions like law enforcement
and the judiciary collapsed.

00:03:54.879 --> 00:04:01.749
But as we heard earlier, two rival administrations
emerged, both claiming to be the real, legitimate

00:04:01.749 --> 00:04:02.749
government.

00:04:02.749 --> 00:04:08.189
There is a legitimacy vacuum in Libya, and
all of the institutions or the governments

00:04:08.189 --> 00:04:14.139
that exist today have had their mandates expire
a long time ago.

00:04:14.139 --> 00:04:21.389
The only reason that these institutions or
these political classes or political leaders

00:04:21.389 --> 00:04:26.520
exist today is because of circumstances, and
it's because of the failure of the democratic

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:29.810
transition.

00:04:29.810 --> 00:04:35.500
What makes it even worse - the country sits
on the division line of a fundamental rivalry

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:38.750
that runs through the entire region.

00:04:38.750 --> 00:04:45.970
The competition for power inside Libya between
domestic players extended more regionally

00:04:45.970 --> 00:04:46.970
and internationally.

00:04:46.970 --> 00:04:52.879
You could also see in this confrontation between
the two camps, two different models of power

00:04:52.879 --> 00:04:55.280
competing in the post Arab Spring era.

00:04:55.280 --> 00:05:03.389
A division between what was called at the
time, the Islamist camp versus the others

00:05:03.389 --> 00:05:10.099
that could be described as nationalist, more
military oriented, et cetera.

00:05:10.099 --> 00:05:17.069
Two broad but distinct models of power: one
largely based on Political Islam and the other

00:05:17.069 --> 00:05:19.800
largely based on miltarist nationalism.

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:27.110
And here is how that has played out in Libya:
The side considered part of the 'militarist

00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:32.240
nationalist' camp is the House of Representatives
and General Khalifa Haftar.

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:38.419
He leads what he calls the Libyan National
Army or LNA and is based in the eastern city

00:05:38.419 --> 00:05:41.490
of Tobruk.

00:05:41.490 --> 00:05:47.430
The LNA is supported by Russia, Saudi Arabia,
the UAE and Egypt.

00:05:47.430 --> 00:05:53.270
Egypt shares a long border with Libya - and
sees Haftar and his LNA as the best way to

00:05:53.270 --> 00:06:00.360
prevent extremist attacks spilling over that
border.

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:05.720
Along with the UAE and Saudi Arabia, Egypt
also sees Haftar as a bulwark against the

00:06:05.720 --> 00:06:12.550
spread Islamic groups like the Muslim Brotherhood
- which they consider a terror organisation.

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:18.889
On the one hand, you have those regional and
international actors that have come to the

00:06:18.889 --> 00:06:27.240
conclusion or to the idea that there's a need
for a strongman that could contribute to restore

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:28.910
authority.

00:06:28.910 --> 00:06:37.060
And so for them, stability, authority and
even military authority are perceived as the

00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:38.070
priority.

00:06:38.070 --> 00:06:44.360
On the other side, you have the UN-recognised
Government of National Accord or GNA.

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:50.040
It is considered to be part of the 'political
Islam' camp, is led by Prime Minister Fayez

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:53.310
al-Sarraj and is based in the capital Tripoli.

00:06:53.310 --> 00:06:58.129
The GNA’s international backers include
Qatar and Turkey.

00:06:58.129 --> 00:07:03.590
Turkey wants drilling rights in the Mediterranean
but also has a shared ideological framework

00:07:03.590 --> 00:07:10.360
with the GNA- namely political Islam and support
for the Muslim Brotherhood across the region.

00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:17.569
The coalition that has brought together the
GNA, Turkey, Qatar represents an attempt to

00:07:17.569 --> 00:07:24.479
bring together those forces that have opposed
military rule, some Islamist element.

00:07:24.479 --> 00:07:29.400
And political Islam has also been an important
component, but not the only one.

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:34.940
You also have European countries like France
and Italy backing opposing sides, each saying

00:07:34.940 --> 00:07:37.139
they want stability in the region.

00:07:37.139 --> 00:07:43.379
But regardless of intention, all of this has
led to a feeling that this isn't really about

00:07:43.379 --> 00:07:46.280
Libyans and their future.

00:07:46.280 --> 00:07:53.499
Sometimes people question whether what we
have in Libya is actually a civil war or an

00:07:53.499 --> 00:07:58.169
international war that's being fought on Libyan
soil and in Libyan skies.

00:07:58.169 --> 00:08:04.199
The rivalry between the two administrations
reached a head in 2019 when Haftar tried but

00:08:04.199 --> 00:08:07.830
failed to conquer Tripoli.

00:08:07.830 --> 00:08:14.310
The country has been at a chaotic impasse
ever since.

00:08:14.310 --> 00:08:18.979
International focus is often on Libya’s
fate as a battleground for regional proxy

00:08:18.979 --> 00:08:20.469
warfare.

00:08:20.469 --> 00:08:24.080
Many Libyan people feel they don’t have
a seat at the table.

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:30.999
We have now gotten to a point where what Libyans
think or what the aspirations of the Libyan

00:08:30.999 --> 00:08:37.010
people are have become irrelevant because
of the level of foreign interference in this

00:08:37.010 --> 00:08:38.010
country.

00:08:38.010 --> 00:08:41.660
Meanwhile daily life in Libya is hard and
is getting worse.

00:08:41.660 --> 00:08:43.089
I have family there.

00:08:43.089 --> 00:08:47.700
I have friends there located on almost all
the geographic locations

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:58.740
I wouldn’t say that they want the same thing
institutionally speaking or that they have

00:08:58.740 --> 00:09:04.839
the same views over legitimacy but mostly
what the Libyan people want is roughly what

00:09:04.839 --> 00:09:13.300
every citizen in this world wants: good governance,
sustainable authorities, employment opportunities,

00:09:13.300 --> 00:09:17.360
some freedom of expression.

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:20.029
They overthrew the regime before.

00:09:20.029 --> 00:09:24.680
If neither of the two administrations which
claim legitimacy fail to improve their lives,

00:09:24.680 --> 00:09:28.839
might the Libyan people have the courage to
challenge both of them?

00:09:28.839 --> 00:09:35.329
We have seen some genuine videos of mothers,
of young people coming out on the streets

00:09:35.329 --> 00:09:41.230
and airing these grievances deteriorating
living conditions, of insecurity, of young

00:09:41.230 --> 00:09:45.740
people being taken to war or leaving the country
on boats to Europe.

00:09:45.740 --> 00:09:53.040
This is an indication that people or the citizens
might have a certain threshold that if that

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:55.200
is crossed, then they will come out on the
streets.

